                    Softmax needs somewhere to park attention mass, and it picks the first few
                    positions — the &ldquo;attention sink&rdquo; effect (StreamingLLM). Their K vectors are
                    also outlier-heavy. Quantizing them aggressively damages every future step&apos;s
                    attention distribution, so implementations routinely keep the first handful of
                    tokens (and the newest 32–128) in FP16. The overhead is constant while the cache
                    grows linearly — at 4,096 tokens, a 128-token FP16 window costs 3% of the cache
                    for most of the accuracy back.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </details>

